;; What to compare, and how far to stretch each fixture per engine.
;;
;; The stretch factors are NOT cosmetic. Each engine's per-frame cost is the
;; SLOPE between a short and a stretched decode, and a slope only resolves a
;; per-frame cost when the extra frames cost more than the invocation's own
;; fixed-cost jitter. Those two engines are four orders of magnitude apart in
;; both quantities, so one shared stretch cannot serve both:
;;
;;   - ffmpeg's fixed cost is process startup (measured ~0.5-1.8 s wall on this
;;     host under load, dominated by dynamic linking, not by decode). Its decode
;;     of a handful of small flat frames is under a millisecond. At stretch 2 the
;;     slope came out NEGATIVE — the second invocation happened to start faster.
;;     ffmpeg therefore needs a stretch in the hundreds before the added decode
;;     work is visible above that.
;;   - utsushi's fixed cost is one already-warm JVM method call. Its per-frame
;;     cost is seconds. A stretch in the hundreds would take the better part of
;;     an hour per fixture, and buys nothing it does not already resolve.
;;
;; Using one stretch for both would mean either an unresolvable ffmpeg number or
;; an unrunnable utsushi one, and the first of those looks exactly like a
;; measurement (see this bench's README).
[{:file "gop32x3.mp4"    :picture "32x32"   :utsushi-stretch 20 :ffmpeg-stretch 200}
 {:file "gop160x128.mp4" :picture "160x128" :utsushi-stretch 5  :ffmpeg-stretch 100}
 {:file "gop320x240.mp4" :picture "320x240" :utsushi-stretch 3  :ffmpeg-stretch 40}]
